@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1769 = private unnamed_addr constant [1019 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for comparisons that test `sys.version` against string literals,\0Asuch that the comparison will evaluate to `False` on Python 3.10 or later.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AComparing `sys.version` to a string is error-prone and may cause subtle\0Abugs, as the comparison will be performed lexicographically, not\0Asemantically. For example, `sys.version > \223.9\22` will evaluate to `False`\0Awhen using Python 3.10, as `\223.10\22` is lexicographically \22less\22 than\0A`\223.9\22`.\0A\0AInstead, use `sys.version_info` to access the current major and minor\0Aversion numbers as a tuple, which can be compared to other tuples\0Awithout issue.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version > \223.9\22  # `False` on Python 3.10.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version_info > (3, 9)  # `True` on Python 3.10.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1770 = private unnamed_addr constant [69 x i8] c"`sys.version` compared to string (python3.10), use `sys.version_info`", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1771 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1770, [8 x i8] c"E\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1772 = private unnamed_addr constant [1070 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for equality comparisons against the major version returned by\0A`sys.version_info` (e.g., `sys.version_info[0] == 3`).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing `sys.version_info[0] == 3` to verify that the major version is\0APython 3 or greater will fail if the major version number is ever\0Aincremented (e.g., to Python 4). This is likely unintended, as code\0Athat uses this comparison is likely intended to be run on Python 2,\0Abut would now run on Python 4 too.\0A\0AInstead, use `>=` to check if the major version number is 3 or greater,\0Ato future-proof the code.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info[0] == 3:\0A    ...\0Aelse:\0A    print(\22Python 2\22)  # This will be printed on Python 4.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info >= (3,):\0A    ...\0Aelse:\0A    print(\22Python 2\22)  # This will not be printed on Python 4.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1773 = private unnamed_addr constant [57 x i8] c"`sys.version_info[0] == 3` referenced (python4), use `>=`", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1774 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1773, [8 x i8] c"9\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1775 = private unnamed_addr constant [902 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for comparisons that test `sys.version_info[1]` against an integer.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AComparisons based on the current minor version number alone can cause\0Asubtle bugs and would likely lead to unintended effects if the Python\0Amajor version number were ever incremented (e.g., to Python 4).\0A\0AInstead, compare `sys.version_info` to a tuple, including the major and\0Aminor version numbers, to future-proof the code.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info[1] < 7:\0A    print(\22Python 3.6 or earlier.\22)  # This will be printed on Python 4.0.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info < (3, 7):\0A    print(\22Python 3.6 or earlier.\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1776 = private unnamed_addr constant [88 x i8] c"`sys.version_info[1]` compared to integer (python4), compare `sys.version_info` to tuple", align 1
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1777 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1776, [8 x i8] c"X\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1778 = private unnamed_addr constant [908 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for comparisons that test `sys.version_info.minor` against an integer.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AComparisons based on the current minor version number alone can cause\0Asubtle bugs and would likely lead to unintended effects if the Python\0Amajor version number were ever incremented (e.g., to Python 4).\0A\0AInstead, compare `sys.version_info` to a tuple, including the major and\0Aminor version numbers, to future-proof the code.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info.minor < 7:\0A    print(\22Python 3.6 or earlier.\22)  # This will be printed on Python 4.0.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info < (3, 7):\0A    print(\22Python 3.6 or earlier.\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
