@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1781 = private unnamed_addr constant [910 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for comparisons that test `sys.version` against string literals,\0Asuch that the comparison would fail if the major version number were\0Aever incremented to Python 10 or higher.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AComparing `sys.version` to a string is error-prone and may cause subtle\0Abugs, as the comparison will be performed lexicographically, not\0Asemantically.\0A\0AInstead, use `sys.version_info` to access the current major and minor\0Aversion numbers as a tuple, which can be compared to other tuples\0Awithout issue.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version >= \223\22  # `False` on Python 10.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport sys\0A\0Asys.version_info >= (3,)  # `True` on Python 10.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version)\0A- [Python documentation: `sys.version_info`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.version_info)\0A", align 1
