@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1851 = private unnamed_addr constant [1140 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unquoted type expressions in `typing.cast()` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThis rule helps enforce a consistent style across your codebase.\0A\0AIt's often necessary to quote the first argument passed to `cast()`,\0Aas type expressions can involve forward references, or references\0Ato symbols which are only imported in `typing.TYPE_CHECKING` blocks.\0AThis can lead to a visual inconsistency across different `cast()` calls,\0Awhere some type expressions are quoted but others are not. By enabling\0Athis rule, you ensure that all type expressions passed to `cast()` are\0Aquoted, enforcing stylistic consistency across all of your `cast()` calls.\0A\0AIn some cases where `cast()` is used in a hot loop, this rule may also\0Ahelp avoid overhead from repeatedly evaluating complex type expressions at\0Aruntime.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import cast\0A\0Ax = cast(dict[str, int], foo)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import cast\0A\0Ax = cast(\22dict[str, int]\22, foo)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is safe as long as the type expression doesn't span multiple\0Alines and includes comments on any of the lines apart from the last one.\0A", align 1
