@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1891 = private unnamed_addr constant [1393 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for single strings assigned to `__slots__`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, the `__slots__` attribute allows you to explicitly define the\0Aattributes (instance variables) that a class can have. By default, Python\0Auses a dictionary to store an object's attributes, which incurs some memory\0Aoverhead. However, when `__slots__` is defined, Python uses a more compact\0Ainternal structure to store the object's attributes, resulting in memory\0Asavings.\0A\0AAny string iterable may be assigned to `__slots__` (most commonly, a\0A`tuple` of strings). If a string is assigned to `__slots__`, it is\0Ainterpreted as a single attribute name, rather than an iterable of attribute\0Anames. This can cause confusion, as users that iterate over the `__slots__`\0Avalue may expect to iterate over a sequence of attributes, but would instead\0Aiterate over the characters of the string.\0A\0ATo use a single string attribute in `__slots__`, wrap the string in an\0Aiterable container type, like a `tuple`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Person:\0A    __slots__: str = \22name\22\0A\0A    def __init__(self, name: str) -> None:\0A        self.name = name\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Person:\0A    __slots__: tuple[str, ...] = (\22name\22,)\0A\0A    def __init__(self, name: str) -> None:\0A        self.name = name\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `__slots__`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#slots)\0A", align 1
