@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1900 = private unnamed_addr constant [727 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__init__` methods that are turned into generators by the\0Ainclusion of `yield` or `yield from` expressions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `__init__` method is the constructor for a given Python class,\0Aresponsible for initializing, rather than creating, new objects.\0A\0AThe `__init__` method has to return `None`. By including a `yield` or\0A`yield from` expression in an `__init__`, the method will return a\0Agenerator object when called at runtime, resulting in a runtime error.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass InitIsGenerator:\0A    def __init__(self, i):\0A        yield i\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [CodeQL: `py-init-method-is-generator`](https://codeql.github.com/codeql-query-help/python/py-init-method-is-generator/)\0A", align 1
