@anon.6aa2d360734573fdde4b4ffe4a284ba1.1929 = private unnamed_addr constant [662 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `__future__` imports.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `__future__` module is used to enable features that are not yet\0Aavailable in the current Python version. If a feature is already\0Aavailable in the minimum supported Python version, importing it\0Afrom `__future__` is unnecessary and should be removed to avoid\0Aconfusion.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom __future__ import print_function\0A\0Aprint(\22Hello, world!\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aprint(\22Hello, world!\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `__future__` \E2\80\94 Future statement definitions](https://docs.python.org/3/library/__future__.html)\0A", align 1
